      "source": "A star can appear red because it is cool or because intervening dust preferentially removes shorter-wavelength light from the direct view. The lecture uses both V/B and B/U comparisons to help correct reddening. A red appearance alone is insufficient to determine temperature. Reflected light from dust can be blue while transmitted starlight is redder.",
      "html": "<ul class=\"study-points\"><li>A star can appear red because it is cool or because intervening <mark class=\"study-term\">dust</mark> preferentially removes shorter-wavelength light from the direct view.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The lecture uses both V/B and B/U comparisons to help correct reddening.</li><li>A red appearance alone is insufficient to determine <mark class=\"study-term\">temperature</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Reflected light from <mark class=\"study-term\">dust</mark> can be blue while transmitted starlight is redder.</li></ul>"
